<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamose</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1550 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamose was the last king of the Seventeenth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. He campaigned north against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> and reached the walls of their capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>, as recorded on his stelae at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>. His successor, probably his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a>, completed the expulsion of the Hyksos and reunited Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamose.jpg" alt="Kamose" width="320" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Merja Attia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coffin_of_King_Kamose_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kamose was the last king of the Theban Seventeenth Dynasty, at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>. His prenomen was Wadjkheperre. He was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seqenenre-tao">Seqenenre Tao</a> and the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>. His mother is unknown but is thought to be Ahhotep I. His father had begun the war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> and died fighting them. Originally the Theban rulers had been at peace with the Hyksos kingdom to the north, controlling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> up to Elephantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> as far north as Cusae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kamose's records on the Carnarvon Tablet, paralleled in his Theban stelae, report that his council doubted the wisdom of war. They argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> up to Cusae was loyal and tranquil, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> grazing in the Delta marshes. The king answered that he could not bear to sit between a chieftain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> and another in Kush, each holding a slice of Egypt, and he vowed to save Egypt. The tablet says he went north by the command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, but this is common to royal inscriptions and does not show a religious motive.</p><p>In his third year he sailed north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and reached Nefrusy, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjay">Medjay</a> troops overran a garrison loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>. His second stela tells how his soldiers captured a courier carrying a message from the Hyksos king Apophis to the ruler of Kush, asking for support. Kamose ordered troops to occupy and destroy the Bahariya Oasis to guard his rear. His third year is the only attested date, but he campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> before and after it. Rock inscriptions at Arminna and Toshka name Kamose and Ahmose side by side, which suggests a coregency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kamose was buried modestly, in an ungilded coffin lacking a royal uraeus, which may imply that he died before his burial equipment was finished. His mummy was found in 1857 at Dra Abu el-Naga, hidden in a pile of debris, in a painted and stuccoed coffin. Buried with it were a gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> dagger, amulets, a scarab, a bronze mirror and a pectoral in the shape of a cartouche with the name of his successor and brother, Ahmose. The coffin remains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the dagger is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and the pectoral and mirror are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kamose” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Kamose</b> campaigned north to the walls of Avaris, and his stelae at Karnak record an intercepted letter from Apepi to the ruler of Kush.</blockquote><a href="/state/seventeenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Seventeenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
